1. What problem does this solve?
A normal tRNA gene sits in one piece in the genome.
A split tRNA gene does not. It is stored as two separate pieces — a 5′ half and a 3′ half — sometimes very far apart. The cell transcribes both pieces and joins them afterwards to make one working tRNA.
Split tRNAs are hard to find, because neither half looks like a tRNA on its own. Our pipeline looks for them.
________________________________________

2. How does it work?
Six simple steps.
Step 1 — Learn what a real tRNA looks like. We take 199 known tRNAs, all lined up at 72 standard positions. At each position we count how often A, C, G and U appear. This gives us a "profile" of a typical tRNA.
Step 2 — Search the genome for halves. We slide along both strands of the M. barkeri genome and note every stretch that looks like a tRNA 5′ half or a tRNA 3′ half.
Step 3 — Join the halves. We pair each 5′ half with each 3′ half and glue them together. The glue point is always position 37/38. This is not a guess — 37/38 is the normal place where archaeal tRNAs carry introns, and it is where real split tRNAs are normally joined. We never used any other position.
Step 4 — Score each joined sequence. We compare it to the profile from Step 1 and give it a score. A high score means "this looks like a real tRNA."
Step 5 — Check against fakes. A score is meaningless on its own. So we shuffle the genome 50 times and run the exact same pipeline on each shuffled copy. Anything it finds there is a false positive by definition. We call these decoys, and we use them to set the pass mark.
Step 6 — Add a GC-content filter. Real tRNA stems are rich in G and C, because G–C pairs are stronger than A–U pairs. Loops are not. We measure this and use it as an extra requirement.
